hadiths[2]{hadithnumber,text}:
"191","On a man finding his property with another: Harun ibn Abdullah narrated to us, Hammad ibn Mas'adah narrated to us, from Ibn Jurayj, from Ikrimah ibn Khalid. Usayd ibn Hudayr ibn Simak narrated to me - Harun said: Ahmad, meaning Ibn Hanbal, said to me: In his book, [meaning Ibn Jurayj's,] it is Usayd ibn Zuhayr, but thus he narrated to them at Basra - that Mu'awiyah wrote to Marwan: If a man finds his stolen property in the hand of a man, he is most entitled to it. So Marwan wrote to me of that while I was over al-Yamamah, and I wrote to him: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) decreed that if he finds it in the hand of a man who is not suspect, he may either take it for what it was bought for, or pursue his thief; and Abu Bakr and Umar decreed likewise after him. So Marwan sent my letter to Mu'awiyah, and Mu'awiyah wrote to Marwan: Neither you nor Usayd shall decree against me in what I have been put in charge of; rather I shall decree against you both, so carry out what I have decreed. So Marwan sent Mu'awiyah's letter to me, and Usayd said: The [Prophet] (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), Abu Bakr, and Umar decreed thus; by Allah, I shall never decree otherwise."
"192","Amr ibn Awn narrated to us, Hushaym informed us, from Musa ibn al-Sa'ib, from Qatadah, from al-Hasan, from Samurah, who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Whoever finds his very property with a man is most entitled to it, and the buyer pursues the one who sold to him."
